WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.420
- [Presenter] Did you know that there are

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:03.780
more than 4 million people

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.700
who live in American territories

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.540
that aren't part of the 50 US states?

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.370
In fact, the US claims 16 territories

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:16.270
outside of the continental United States,

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:20.420
although a few of those are in
dispute with other countries

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:24.690
and only five of them are
permanently inhabited.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.110
So where are these territories?

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.260
Let's look at a map.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.930
Most of the territories
are here in the Pacific.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:36.660
The inhabited territories here are Guam,

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.910
the Northern Mariana
Islands and American Samoa.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:44.800
Then there are two here in the Caribbean,

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.470
the US Virgin Islands and Puerto Rico.

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:51.040
You may also be surprised to learn

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.820
that the capital of the
United States, Washington,

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.440
is in a special geographic
and political zone

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.440
called the District of Columbia,
which is also not a state.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:07.840
The most populous territory
by far is Puerto Rico

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.180
with more than 3 million residents.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:15.870
Its population is greater
than 19 of the US states,

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:20.870
putting it just behind Utah
but ahead of Iowa in numbers.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.060
The District of Columbia has
more than 700,000 residents,

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:30.410
putting it ahead of just
two states in population.

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:34.020
The other four territories
are much smaller

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.770
with between 50,000 and 200,000 residents.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:41.840
The citizenship status and voting rights

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.130
of the people living in these territories

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:47.720
might be different from
residents of the states.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:51.700
Let's delve a little bit
more into these differences.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.780
Most people living in
the American territories

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.010
are granted US citizenship at birth,

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:01.830
although that has not
always been the case.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:05.440
Although white people born
in the District of Columbia

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.230
have had citizenship at birth

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:10.470
from the creation of the district in 1790,

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.830
the large black population of the city

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:15.110
didn't gain citizenship protections

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:19.570
until the passage of the
14th Amendment in 1868.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:21.640
Over the course of the 20th century,

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.540
the residents of four
of the other territories

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.240
have gotten citizenship
status by various means,

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.710
starting with Puerto Rico by
an act of Congress in 1917.

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:36.910
The US Virgin Islands gained citizenship

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:40.220
by congressional order in 1927.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.790
The 1952 Immigration and
Nationality Act expanded

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.930
the definition of who received
US citizenship at birth

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:49.870
to include Guam.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.650
And residents of the
Northern Mariana Islands

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.670
became citizens when their territory

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:58.320
became a Commonwealth in 1976.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.810
The residents of American Samoa
are currently US nationals,

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.050
not US citizens.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:07.810
This means that they
have the right to travel

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:09.820
and live in the United States.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:12.670
And they're afforded
protection by the United States

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.270
when traveling abroad,

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:18.300
but they don't qualify for other
benefits of US citizenship,

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.220
like the right to vote.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.620
But that might change soon.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:26.070
Right now, a case which
is seeking US citizenship

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.010
for American Samoans,

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:30.710
Fitisemanu versus the United States,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.470
is making its way through
the federal court system.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:36.973
So we may hear a ruling
on this case this year.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.130
The citizens of the territories
and Washington DC have many

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:46.050
of the same citizenship
rights and responsibilities

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.560
as citizens of the states.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.460
Remember, because American
Samoans are US nationals

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:53.830
rather than citizens,

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.118
their situation is a little bit different.

00:03:56.118 --> 00:03:58.750
So what I'm about to say
does not apply to them.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:01.310
The citizens of the
territories have the right

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:05.690
to move freely within the United
States without a passport.

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:09.830
They also have the right to
vote in presidential primaries

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:12.510
but not in presidential elections.

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:14.630
We'll talk more about
voting rights in a sec,

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:18.290
because DC is also a bit
different in this regard.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:19.770
Citizens in the territories

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:23.180
also have most of the same
civil and political rights

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:24.950
as the people in the states,

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:28.700
and their responsibilities
are also much the same.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:31.630
Although they don't have
to pay federal income tax,

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:33.670
they do have to pay other federal taxes

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:36.150
like Social Security and Medicare.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:39.620
They also must defend the
country when called upon

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.450
and obey federal laws and regulations.

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:45.680
However, even though the citizens

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.380
of the territories and Washington DC have

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.670
to obey federal laws,

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:54.900
pay federal taxes and defend
the country when called upon,

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.300
they do not have any voting
representation in Congress.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.630
Each of the territories has a delegate

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:03.620
in the House of Representatives.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.430
But although those delegates
may participate and debate

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:12.430
and sit on committees,
they may not cast votes.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:15.280
The citizens of Puerto Rico, Guam,

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:16.780
the US Virgin Islands

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:19.960
and the Northern Mariana Islands can vote

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:21.870
for their territorial governor,

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:26.720
their delegate in the House
and in presidential primaries.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.480
The citizens of Washington DC can vote

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:32.010
for their mayor and City Council,

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.090
although Congress has the authority

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:36.760
to block any laws passed by that council,

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:38.410
if they disagree with them.

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:42.800
DC citizens may vote in
presidential elections as well.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.210
The 23rd Amendment to the US
Constitution, ratified in 1961,

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.740
gave the district three votes
in the electoral college

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:55.540
for the election of the
president and the vice president.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:59.000
So what do you think about
citizenship and voting rights

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.180
in the territories and in
the District of Columbia?

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:04.360
How are they different from those

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:06.240
of the citizens of the states?

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:07.920
And how are they similar?

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.750
Why do you think they're different?

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:11.403
And do you think they should be?

